# Bazel overrides for ONE checkout. `sh tools/setup.sh` writes .bazelrc.local
# for you; this file is for writing it by hand instead. Copy it to
# .bazelrc.local (which is NOT tracked: the right path differs per machine and
# per user) and edit to taste. Every clone and every worktree reads its own.
#
# WHY: a full run of this repo builds ~1300 test binaries plus 84 ASan twins.
# Measured footprint under the output root: 8.9 GB. A 42 campus machine usually
# caps $HOME at 5 GB and gives you no sudo, so the build fails partway with a
# disk error that does not name the cause.
#
# Without this file the root is not unset: tools/bazel, the wrapper bazelisk
# runs, picks the same one setup.sh would (/goinfre/$USER/bazel on campus,
# ~/.cache/42-piscine/bazel elsewhere). `bazel info output_base` says which:
# the root is the folder above it.
#
# Point Bazel's ENTIRE state somewhere with room. This one flag covers the
# output base, the install base and the repository cache; there is no need to
# set them separately.
#
# Use a path that is yours alone, with your login in it: /tmp is shared, and
# two students pointing at the same directory fight over the same lock.
startup --output_user_root=/tmp/CHANGEME-your-login/bazel

# One more lives in your shell profile rather than here, because it is an
# ENVIRONMENT variable and not a Bazel flag, and it still lands under $HOME
# by default:
#
#     export BAZELISK_HOME=/tmp/bazelisk-$USER
#
#         Bazelisk downloads Bazel itself (~63 MB) to ~/.cache/bazelisk.
